# Configuration for provision-hetzner.sh. Copy to watch.env beside this file
# and fill in; watch.env is gitignored because HCLOUD_TOKEN is a credential
# that can create and destroy servers.
#
#   cp watch.env.example watch.env
#
# QUOTE ANY VALUE CONTAINING A SPACE, or use commas. This file is sourced by
# the shell, where `SSH_KEY=one two` is the "set a variable for one command"
# syntax — it assigns `one` and then tries to run `two`, which fails with
# "command not found" and aborts. `SSH_KEY="one two"` and `SSH_KEY=one,two`
# both work.

# Read+write API token, from the Hetzner console under Security -> API tokens.
# Read-only is not enough: the script creates a server.
HCLOUD_TOKEN=

# Names or numeric ids of SSH keys already uploaded to the Hetzner project,
# separated by spaces or commas.
#
# Pass every key you will ever want. Hetzner injects SSH keys at creation
# only — there is no API call that adds one to a running server, so
# afterwards it means appending to authorized_keys over a session you still
# have, and losing every key means rescue mode. At minimum pass a working key
# and a recovery key kept somewhere else; add a second administrator now if
# one is ever likely, because retrofitting is the painful path. The script
# warns if only one key is given.
#
#   SSH_KEY="epicurrents-evidence-admin epicurrents-evidence-recovery"
SSH_KEY=

# Name or numeric id of a Hetzner firewall, applied in the create call.
#
# Required, and not a formality: a server created without one answers SSH from
# the whole internet from the moment it boots, and this host exists to be
# unreachable. Create it in the console before the first run with:
#   inbound  — TCP 22 from your own address only, nothing else
#   outbound — allow all (Tailscale, apt, alert delivery all need it)
# Tailscale connects outbound, so no inbound port is needed for the tailnet
# itself; leaving UDP 41641 closed just forces traffic through a DERP relay,
# which for a log shipper is fine. Narrow inbound 22 further, or close it
# entirely, once the machine is on the tailnet.
FIREWALL=

# Optional: push notifications to https://ntfy.sh/<topic> while watching.
NTFY_TOPIC=

# Set to 1 to create the server the moment one becomes available. This starts
# billing, possibly at three in the morning, and the script exits after one
# success.
AUTO_CREATE=0

# Defaults are cax11 and cx23 in hel1/fsn1/nbg1 — all EEA, cheapest first,
# cax11 being ARM64 and cx23 its x86 equivalent (every image in the stack
# publishes both). Those names are perishable: Hetzner renames its lines
# between generations, so the script resolves each one at startup and tells
# you which no longer exist rather than watching for a type that cannot be
# found. What matters is the shape — shared vCPU, at least 2 cores and 4 GB.
#
# A non-EEA location is refused unless ALLOW_NON_EEA=1, which is a decision
# about the privacy notice rather than a convenience.
#TYPES="cax11 cx23"
#LOCATIONS="hel1 fsn1"

# Resume only. A Volume cannot exist before its first server, so leave this
# unset on the first run, then create a Volume in the same location for the
# Borg repository and set its id here.
#VOLUME_ID=

#SERVER_NAME=epicurrents-evidence
#IMAGE=ubuntu-24.04
#INTERVAL=60
